{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1788201281,"nonce":"rjMuuhGTG5jxQfiG18fxjn7nC3Q=","target":"4087644234166978de87523c80c894beebc5bff1ebe24fc2373410f5a3a559b8","message":"Found by survey after a live incident, 2026-08-31. Evan's framing: \"when you\nassign a pawn to do something, what if they just happen to do it and your\nassignment did nothing? that's a false positive right?\"\n\nYes. And there is a confirmed mechanism, not just a theoretical worry.\n\nTHE CONFIRMED BUG\n\n`Verse.AI/Pawn_JobTracker.cs`, `TryTakeOrderedJob`:\n\n    job.playerForced = true;\n    if (curJob != null \u0026\u0026 curJob.JobIsSameAs(pawn, job))\n    {\n        return true;\n    }\n\nIf the pawn is ALREADY running an equivalent job, the call returns `true`\nhaving done nothing. Our `Job` instance — the one carrying `playerForced` — is\ndiscarded, and `curJob` keeps `playerForced == false`.\n\nAll 17 job-ordering verbs then report `counts.accepted:1` for a call that\nchanged nothing: `move-to`, `attack`, `prioritize`, `rescue`, `capture`,\n`arrest`, `carry`, `equip`, `wear`, `drop`, `consume`, `extinguish`,\n`beat-fire`, `tend`, `repair`, `man-turret`, `rest-until-healed`.\n\n`job_def` in the result does not save us: it is read off `pawn.CurJobDef` AFTER\nthe call, so it re-observes whatever the pawn is doing — which in the collision\ncase is the job we did NOT cause.\n\nHOW IT BIT US\n\nMarine armour was force-`wear`n on four pawns whose apparel policy independently\nwanted the same armour. `wear` returned `accepted:1` for all of them. Three ended\nup armoured, one did not, and there was no way to tell which mechanism dressed\nwhom. I then invented an explanation (the policy fighting the order) and stated\nit as fact without verifying it. That is the real cost: the agent builds a wrong\nmodel of its own effectiveness and acts on it.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE FOR AN AGENT THAN A PLAYER\n\nA human watches the screen. An agent has only the result envelope, so \"accepted\"\nis its entire evidence that an action worked. If accepted can mean \"you changed\nnothing\", then every did-my-action-work check is confounded and the agent cannot\nlearn which of its actions are effective.\n\nFIX — small, no Harmony, no per-tick cost\n\n1. **The honesty fix.** In every (b) verb, test\n   `curJob != null \u0026\u0026 curJob.JobIsSameAs(pawn, job)` BEFORE calling\n   `TryTakeOrderedJob`, and report it as a distinct outcome —\n   `gate:\"already-doing-it\"` — not as `accepted`.\n2. **Publish the attribution fields** on `PawnActs.JobLine()` and\n   `PawnSerializer`'s `state`, all plain public field reads:\n   `job_id` (`Job.loadID`), `player_forced` (`Job.playerForced`),\n   `job_giver` (`Job.jobGiver?.GetType().Name` — reads `ThinkNode_QueuedJob`\n   for an ordered job vs the JobGiver type for a think-tree job, which is a\n   live discriminator), `work_giver` (`Job.workGiverDef?.defName`),\n   `job_start_tick`.\n3. **Publish the job QUEUE** (`pawn.jobs.AllJobs()`). With `queue:true`,\n   `TryTakeOrderedJob` enqueues and leaves `curJob` alone, so today's `job_def`\n   names the PREVIOUS job and the ordered one is invisible.\n4. **`forced` on apparel rows.** `RimWorld/JobDriver_Wear.cs`'s final toil does\n   `forcedHandler.SetForced(apparel, true)` when `job.playerForced`, so\n   `Pawn_OutfitTracker.forcedHandler.ForcedApparel` is a PERSISTED, per-item\n   completion receipt — \"this is worn BECAUSE it was forced\" — that survives\n   save/load. `PawnSerializer.Apparel()` publishes no such field and nothing in\n   the repo reads it. It is the one verb of the 17 with a durable trace, and it\n   is exactly the verb that bit us.\n   HAZARD: read `ForcedApparel` and `Contains`, NOT `IsForced(ap)` — the latter\n   `Log.Error`s and mutates the list when the apparel is destroyed.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE HERE. A general `job_end` journal event was surveyed and\ndeliberately deferred: it misses the early-out above entirely (no job is taken,\nso there is nothing to correlate), fires spuriously on job suspend, misses the\ndeath/despawn/draft paths that go through `StopAll`, and its per-tick cost is\nunmeasured. Fix the false positive first; the general question is a separate\nconversation.\n\nAcceptance\n\n- A `wear`/`equip`/`move-to` order issued to a pawn ALREADY doing that job is\n  reported as `already-doing-it`, not `accepted`, AND STILL WRITES AN\n  `action` row carrying that verdict. **Corrected 2026-08-31 (session 9).**\n  This bullet originally said \"writes no `action` row\", which directly\n  contradicted comment #1 below. Comment #1 supersedes: it is later, and it\n  names the actual cost — the wasted orders are exactly the ones invisible to\n  the journal, and the journal is the aggregate the agent learns from, so\n  suppressing the row makes \"which of my instructions are redundant\"\n  unanswerable at session end no matter how good the per-call reporting is.\n  The contradiction was caught by the vetting worker before any code was\n  written; it would otherwise have decided the shape of the fix by coin-flip.\n- `job_id`, `player_forced` and `job_giver` appear on the job line and on\n  `pawn`'s state.\n- `queue:true` reports the job that was queued, not the one still running.\n- An apparel row shows `forced:true` for a completed forced wear and `false`\n  for the same apparel worn by the pawn's own policy — demonstrated live with\n  both cases on one bench.","files":null}]}